---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import EmailCapture from '../components/EmailCapture.astro';
---
<Base title="The Colorado Elopement Planning Checklist | Colorado Elopement Guide"
  description="Step-by-step Colorado elopement planning: marriage license, self-solemnization, permits for RMNP and other public lands, vendor booking order, and timeline.">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-5 md:px-8 pt-14">
    <p class="eyebrow text-clay-600 mb-3">Free planning resource</p>
    <h1 class="display text-4xl md:text-5xl leading-tight">The Colorado Elopement <span class="serif-i">Checklist</span></h1>
    <p class="text-ink/65 mt-4 leading-relaxed">Everything below links to the official source where one exists. Rules and fees change — always confirm with the office listed before you book travel.</p>

    <section class="mt-8 rounded-3xl border border-hairline bg-white p-7">
      <h2 class="display text-xl">1. The legal part (it's easier here than almost anywhere)</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-ink/75 list-disc pl-5">
        <li><strong>Marriage license:</strong> apply together at any Colorado county clerk &amp; recorder — the license works anywhere in the state. Confirm current fees, ID requirements and validity window with the clerk you'll use (e.g. <a href="https://denvergov.org/Government/Agencies-Departments-Offices/Agencies-Departments-Offices-Directory/Clerk-and-Recorder" rel="noopener" class="underline">Denver Clerk &amp; Recorder</a>, or the clerk in the county you're marrying in).</li>
        <li><strong>Self-solemnization:</strong> Colorado law (C.R.S. § 14-2-109) lets couples solemnize their own marriage — no officiant required. You can also have a friend, an officiant, or a judge. If it's just the two of you on a mountain, that's legal here.</li>
        <li><strong>After the ceremony:</strong> sign and return the license to the issuing clerk for recording within the window they specify.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-5 rounded-3xl border border-hairline bg-white p-7">
      <h2 class="display text-xl">2. Location &amp; permits</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-ink/75 list-disc pl-5">
        <li><strong>Rocky Mountain National Park:</strong> ceremonies require a special use permit from the park, with designated ceremony sites and group-size limits — apply early, popular dates go fast. Official info: <a href="https://www.nps.gov/romo/planyourvisit/weddings.htm" rel="noopener" class="underline">nps.gov/romo — weddings</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Garden of the Gods:</strong> permits run through the City of Colorado Springs parks department — see the City of Colorado Springs parks department: <a href="https://coloradosprings.gov/parks" rel="noopener" class="underline">coloradosprings.gov</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Maroon Bells, Sapphire Point &amp; other USFS sites:</strong> many popular Forest Service spots require reservations or special use permits, and some restrict group sizes and decor — find your ranger district at <a href="https://www.fs.usda.gov" rel="noopener" class="underline">fs.usda.gov</a> for the ranger district that manages your site.</li>
        <li><strong>Private venues:</strong> no permit needed — the venue handles it. Browse <a href="/category/venues" class="underline">micro-wedding venues</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Leave No Trace:</strong> public-land ceremonies generally can't include things like confetti, released flowers/balloons, or moved vegetation — your photographer or planner will know the rules for your exact spot.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-5 rounded-3xl border border-hairline bg-white p-7">
      <h2 class="display text-xl">3. Booking order (what to lock first)</h2>
      <ol class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-ink/75 list-decimal pl-5">
        <li><a href="/category/photographers" class="underline">Photographer</a> — elopement specialists book 6–12 months out and most know the permit process cold; many will help you choose the spot.</li>
        <li>Location + permit (or <a href="/category/venues" class="underline">venue</a>).</li>
        <li><a href="/category/officiants" class="underline">Officiant</a> — or decide to self-solemnize.</li>
        <li><a href="/category/planners" class="underline">Planner</a> if you want logistics handled — especially for winter or remote sites.</li>
        <li><a href="/category/florists" class="underline">Florals</a> and <a href="/category/hair-makeup" class="underline">hair &amp; makeup</a> — many artists travel to trailheads and hotels.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-5 rounded-3xl border border-hairline bg-white p-7">
      <h2 class="display text-xl">4. Questions to ask every vendor</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-ink/75 list-disc pl-5">
        <li>Have you worked at our specific location, and do you know its permit and access rules?</li>
        <li>What happens if weather forces a date or location change?</li>
        <li>What exactly is included, what costs extra, and when are payments due?</li>
        <li>Do you carry insurance the venue/land manager requires?</li>
        <li>For photographers: how do you handle sunrise/sunset timing and hiking distances?</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-5 rounded-3xl border border-hairline bg-white p-7">
      <h2 class="display text-xl">5. Mountain-specific reality checks</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-ink/75 list-disc pl-5">
        <li>Altitude is real: plan an acclimatization day if you're coming from sea level.</li>
        <li>Afternoon thunderstorms are routine June–August — sunrise ceremonies dodge both storms and crowds.</li>
        <li>Snow can close high-country access from roughly October through late June; ask your photographer what's actually reachable on your date.</li>
        <li>Cell service is spotty — print your permit, license and vendor contacts.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <div class="mt-8"><EmailCapture /></div>
  </div>
</Base>
